@@s001 Examples: map, distribute and map2 in Scala
@@s002 <span>Week 9 · examples</span>
@@s003 Examples: map, distribute and map2 in Scala
@@s004 The code of the <a href="../teoria/s09-functor.html">functor page</a>, with <code>map</code> on List and Option, the identity law, <code>distribute</code> and <code>map2</code>. Checked with Scala 3.8.4 and the course options.
@@s005 The whole file
@@s006 Run it with <code>scala-cli run Functor.scala</code>. <code>distribute</code> is written for lists, <code>map2</code> for Options: they are the concrete versions of the concept.
@@s007 Functor.scala
@@s008 <code>distribute</code>. <code>fab.map(_._1)</code> takes the first element of each pair, <code>_._2</code> the second. The result is a pair of lists. <code>_</code> is the placeholder for the current element of the lambda.
@@s009 <code>map2</code> with <code>flatMap</code>. If <code>fa</code> is None the result is None without running the rest. Otherwise, in the lambda <code>a =&gt; …</code>, it takes the value <code>a</code>, then with <code>fb.map</code> combines it with <code>b</code> using <code>f</code>.
@@s010 <code>val doppio</code> is a function: <code>_ * 2</code> is the short form of <code>x =&gt; x * 2</code>. It will be the f of <code>map</code>.
@@s011 The same <code>map</code> on the three containers: List, Option with a value, empty Option. The second produces <code>Some(6)</code>, the third <code>None</code>: the shape does not change.
@@s012 Identity law: <code>identity</code> returns the argument unchanged, so <code>map(identity)</code> gives a container equal to the input. The comparison <code>==</code> answers <code>true</code>.
@@s013 A list of pairs (name, age) passed to <code>distribute</code>: the result is a pair of lists, the first of the names and the second of the ages.
@@s014 Two Options with a value: <code>map2</code> applies the lambda <code>(n, e) =&gt; s"$n ($e)"</code> and returns <code>Some("John (35)")</code>. <code>s"…"</code> is string interpolation: <code>$n</code> inserts the value of n.
@@s015 Same code with a missing age: <code>Option.empty[Int]</code> is None. The result is None: if a value is missing, <code>map2</code> has nothing to combine.
@@s016 The output
@@s017 Output of the run
@@s018 <code>map</code> on List, full Option and empty Option.
@@s019 Identity law on List and on Option: <code>true</code> in both cases.
@@s020 <code>distribute</code>: a pair of lists.
@@s021 <code>map2</code>: with both values, the result is <code>Some</code>; with a missing value, <code>None</code>.
@@s022 To remember
@@s023 Sentence for the exam
@@s024 A functor is a type constructor with a map operation that preserves the structure: mapping the identity changes nothing.
@@s025 Practical rule
@@s026 A <code>map</code> on the course's containers is always the same shape: if you have two containers to combine, <code>map</code> is not enough, you need <code>map2</code> (and its form with <code>flatMap</code>).
